{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_asmiwd6xknw3mpshxxylesqtkppwi6c6ze63o3j7fs33qg4lhraq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_0182jmuhGVuK5nq1taYzZmw2","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Audit C vs Rust semantic diff","subagent_type":"Explore","prompt":"You are auditing a C-to-Rust translation for EXACT behavioural equivalence. Working directory: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-pfRjrv\n\nRead these two files IN FULL:\n- c_src/src/lib.c  (645 lines, the ground truth — a cute_c2/tinyc2 derived 2D collision library)\n- translation/src/lib.rs (the Rust translation)\n\nYour ONLY job is to report semantic differences. Do NOT edit any file.\n\nFor EVERY function in lib.c, compare against its Rust counterpart and check:\n1. Same control flow: identical `if`/`else if` chain ORDER, identical comparison\n   operators (`<` vs `<=`, `>` vs `>=`), identical `switch` case-to-code mapping\n   including fall-through and which cases share the `default:` label.\n   Note especially: `c2GJKSimplexMetric` (`default:` falls into `case 1:`),\n   `c2L` (has `case 1`, `case 2`, `default` — NO `case 3`), `c2D` (`case 3` and\n   `default` share code), `c2Witness` (cases 1,2,3 + default), `c2MakeProxy`\n   (NO default label at all), `c2Collided` (nested switches with argument\n   SWAPPING in some arms).\n2. Same arithmetic expressions and the same order of floating point operations.\n   The Rust uses helpers `addss(dst,src)`, `subss(dst,src)`, `mulss(dst,src)`,\n   `divss(dst,src)`, `fneg(x)` that emulate x86 SSE. For each, check the\n   VALUE computed is the same as C (ignore which operand is \"dst\"; that models\n   NaN payload choice only). e.g. `subss(a,b)` == C's `a - b`, `divss(a,b)` ==\n   C's `a / b`. Report any case where the Rust computes a mathematically\n   DIFFERENT expression than the C (wrong operand for a non-commutative op,\n   missing negation, swapped struct field, wrong array index, etc.).\n3. Same struct field reads/writes, same indices. The C uses\n   `c2sv *verts = &s.a;` and indexes `verts[i]`; the Rust models\n   `c2Simplex { verts: [c2sv;4], div, count }`. Check `s->a`==verts[0],\n   `s->b`==verts[1], `s->c`==verts[2], `s->d`==verts[3] consistently.\n4. Same assignment ORDER where it matters (e.g. `c23`'s `s->a = s->b; s->b = s->c;`\n   vs `s->b = s->a; s->a = s->c;` — these are order-sensitive aliasing copies).\n5. Same loop bounds and same early-exit (`break`) placement, especially in\n   `c2GJK`'s main while loop: whether `++s.count` happens before or after the\n   duplicate-vertex `break`, and whether the new vertex is written before the\n   dup test.\n6. Same constants (FLT_MAX = 3.402823466e38, FLT_EPSILON = 1.19209289550781250e-7,\n   the squared epsilon, -1.0e8f, 0.5f, 1.0f, 2.0f, and the hard-coded shapes in\n   `aabb()`: circle (-70,0) r20; aabb (-40,-40)..(-15,-15); capsule\n   (-40,40)-(-20,100) r10).\n7. Same integer/bit operations in `c2AABBtoAABB` (`!(d0|d1|d2|d3)`) and `aabb()`\n   (`+=` with `<< 1` and `<< 2`).\n8. `c2GJK`'s cache validation: `if (!(min_metric < max_metric * 2.0f && metric < -1.0e8f)) cache_was_read = 1;`\n   and the min/max ternaries `metric < metric_old ? metric : metric_old` /\n   `metric > metric_old ? metric : metric_old`.\n\nReport your findings as a numbered list. For each finding give: the C line\nnumber(s), the Rust line number(s), the C code, the Rust code, and whether it is\na REAL behavioural difference or benign. If you find NO real differences, say so\nexplicitly and list the functions you verified. Be thorough and skeptical — the\npoint is to find bugs, not to confirm the translation is fine."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":856,"cache_read_input_tokens":283129,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":856,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_man